(7414975, 1168764, 18197, '<td valign="top">Love the processing on this and especially the color treatment. Doesn''t hurt to have been shot in paradise.</td>', 'Love the processing on this and especially the color treatment. Doesn''t hurt to have been shot in paradise.', '2015-11-08 15:52:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(7414978, 1168583, 47974, '<td valign="top"><img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=59029" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">snaffles</a> Thank you for the critique.  I am careful around my gators, not suggesting anyone do what I do without lots of experience being around them (they are wild and dangerous after all).  Unprovoked gator attacks are highly unusual as they tend to leave people alone.  90% of the attacks are from gators that have come to associate people with food because people think it''s cute to feed the gators.  Unfortunately this always results in the gator having to be taken care of, yes this means killed.  This is also one of the reasons you should not clean your fish at the lake and throw the remains in the water, gators don''t take long to find places that people do this and this also leads to their association of people and food.<br/> <br/>In this photo he was bellowing at me.  <a href="//www.rrcolejrphotography.com/img/s8/v78/o1005697182/c1/p1672798068-200.mp4" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">Here</a> is a video I took of a different gator bellowing.  They don''t always open and close their mouth but the up and down action is part of the bellow and that is what this gator was doing when I took the photograph.  I also have the color version and it may show better that he was a wild gator, not sure.  I went black and white because I had not done a B&amp;W edit of a gator shot in awhile and I like how it shows the texture of these wonderful and amazing animals.<br/> <br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=1169551" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/45000-49999/47974/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1169551.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/45000-49999/47974/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1169551.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/45000-49999/47974/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1169551.jpg"/></a> <br/> <br/><a href="//www.rrcolejrphotography.com/alligators" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">Here</a> is a link to my gallery of gator shots<br/> <br/></td>', ' snaffles Thank you for the critique.  I am careful around my gators, not suggesting anyone do what I do without lots of experience being around them (they are wild and dangerous after all).  Unprovoked gator attacks are highly unusual as they tend to leave people alone.  90% of the attacks are from gators that have come to associate people with food because people think it''s cute to feed the gators.  Unfortunately this always results in the gator having to be taken care of, yes this means killed.  This is also one of the reasons you should not clean your fish at the lake and throw the remains in the water, gators don''t take long to find places that people do this and this also leads to their association of people and food. In this photo he was bellowing at me.  Here is a video I took of a different gator bellowing.  They don''t always open and close their mouth but the up and down action is part of the bellow and that is what this gator was doing when I took the photograph.  I also have the color version and it may show better that he was a wild gator, not sure.  I went black and white because I had not done a B&W edit of a gator shot in awhile and I like how it shows the texture of these wonderful and amazing animals.   Here is a link to my gallery of gator shots ', '2015-11-08 16:56:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(7414986, 1167806, 98339, '<td valign="top">I gave you an 8 as I know waterfalls are never appreciated on this site...I don''t know why because capturing them isn''t a walk in the park!!!<br/> <br/>Here is my thoughts (run, run now...)<br/> <br/>To me the waterfall is secondary because it is lost in the BG.  The rocks in the front are more prominate and not as interesting.  If it was my picture (wink) I would have "tried" cropping the top tier of the rocks out (right side - top) and cutting most of the first rock on the left out.  Then I would have played with the shadows more because it would be pretty dark...possibly some dodge n burn???  Buy cropping it you would be bringing the waterfall forward and putting it in a better position IMO.</td>', 'I gave you an 8 as I know waterfalls are never appreciated on this site...I don''t know why because capturing them isn''t a walk in the park!!! Here is my thoughts (run, run now...) To me the waterfall is secondary because it is lost in the BG.  The rocks in the front are more prominate and not as interesting.  If it was my picture (wink) I would have "tried" cropping the top tier of the rocks out (right side - top) and cutting most of the first rock on the left out.  Then I would have played with the shadows more because it would be pretty dark...possibly some dodge n burn???  Buy cropping it you would be bringing the waterfall forward and putting it in a better position IMO.', '2015-11-08 17:20:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(7414989, 1168583, 98339, '<td valign="top">Ok Ronnie here''s my comment....<br/> <br/>you shot this with an f/4???  To SDOF IMO you need at least an 7.1/8  - I should talk because my entries (3) have all been 5.3/5.6....rolling my eyes...<br/> <br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=1109422" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/1000-1999/1911/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1109422.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/1000-1999/1911/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1109422.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/1000-1999/1911/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1109422.jpg"/></a> <br/> <br/>I think that the voters expect to see at least the snout and all the way to the eyes clearly...that was my first thought about your entry.  I don''t mind beyond the eyes but the foreground and mid-section needs to be clear (and yes, I''m going to take my own advice...grin)  We are going back to this river in Apr so fingers crossed I''ll get an even better shot!!!<br/> <br/>FWIW ~ I''ve sharpened the pants off mine and yes, I was on the river bank about 15'' away.  It was past dark and I was using my flash to get the reflection in the eye...grin...it worked!!!<br/> <br/>ETA:<br/> <br/>I also think some dodge &amp; burn would really improve the tones...to me they are a bit flat, not nice and rich...<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2015-11-08 17:31:46.</i></td>', 'Ok Ronnie here''s my comment.... you shot this with an f/4???  To SDOF IMO you need at least an 7.1/8  - I should talk because my entries (3) have all been 5.3/5.6....rolling my eyes...   I think that the voters expect to see at least the snout and all the way to the eyes clearly...that was my first thought about your entry.  I don''t mind beyond the eyes but the foreground and mid-section needs to be clear (and yes, I''m going to take my own advice...grin)  We are going back to this river in Apr so fingers crossed I''ll get an even better shot!!! FWIW ~ I''ve sharpened the pants off mine and yes, I was on the river bank about 15'' away.  It was past dark and I was using my flash to get the reflection in the eye...grin...it worked!!! ETA: I also think some dodge & burn would really improve the tones...to me they are a bit flat, not nice and rich...', '2015-11-08 17:30:45.000000', '2015-11-08 17:31:46.000000', 0),
(7414991, 1168583, 114225, '<td valign="top">The focus on the eyes is great. But it would be even better if the nose was in focus.<br/>I like the black and white, it works well with the detail.<br/>Seems like if you crop the bottom closer to the gator, that would put even more attention on the beast.</td>', 'The focus on the eyes is great. But it would be even better if the nose was in focus.I like the black and white, it works well with the detail.Seems like if you crop the bottom closer to the gator, that would put even more attention on the beast.', '2015-11-08 17:36:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(7414992, 1168974, 114225, '<td valign="top">What stuck out to me as I voted, and even more as I see the original, is the unrealistic clouds. The lights and darks simply don''t add up.<br/>I like that you cropped the bottom, but you cropped a little too much. That big rock ad the end of the rock is slightly chopped off, and looks better if we can see some water in front of it.<br/>The colors in the trees are really dull. Try turning up the brightness of yellows, and the leaves will start to pop.</td>', 'What stuck out to me as I voted, and even more as I see the original, is the unrealistic clouds. The lights and darks simply don''t add up.I like that you cropped the bottom, but you cropped a little too much. That big rock ad the end of the rock is slightly chopped off, and looks better if we can see some water in front of it.The colors in the trees are really dull. Try turning up the brightness of yellows, and the leaves will start to pop.', '2015-11-08 17:41:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(7414993, 1168583, 159723, '<td valign="top">I saw your comment request. Not sure if you also want feedback from someone who is hardly dry behind the ears, but here is my two cent :)<br/> <br/>I agree with Snaffles that the isolation of the eyes is great. I hope I never have to look at a similar expression in real life, unless I am looking through binoculars standing more than 2000 feet away :) I too would have liked to see the nose more in focus. <br/> <br/>I am by no means an expert on b&amp;w, and I might be completely wrong here, but I think I would have preferred a tiny bit more contrast in this images. To be honest, I think I actually prefer the color version. Sorry! The warm yellow on the bottom jaw gives the images vibrance and the weed on the top jaw makes the animal look more wild, in my eyes. To be honest I did assume this was a zoo shot when I saw it during voting. Had a look at your gator gallery. I particularly liked the first one, because there was more of the environment included in the images. There where several images in your gallery that I really liked.<br/> <br/>It was interesting to read your comments. Will watch the video when I''m not about to go to bed :) I might get nightmares!<br/> <br/><br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2015-11-08 17:50:33.</i></td>', 'I saw your comment request. Not sure if you also want feedback from someone who is hardly dry behind the ears, but here is my two cent :) I agree with Snaffles that the isolation of the eyes is great. I hope I never have to look at a similar expression in real life, unless I am looking through binoculars standing more than 2000 feet away :) I too would have liked to see the nose more in focus.  I am by no means an expert on b&w, and I might be completely wrong here, but I think I would have preferred a tiny bit more contrast in this images. To be honest, I think I actually prefer the color version. Sorry! The warm yellow on the bottom jaw gives the images vibrance and the weed on the top jaw makes the animal look more wild, in my eyes. To be honest I did assume this was a zoo shot when I saw it during voting. Had a look at your gator gallery. I particularly liked the first one, because there was more of the environment included in the images. There where several images in your gallery that I really liked. It was interesting to read your comments. Will watch the video when I''m not about to go to bed :) I might get nightmares! ', '2015-11-08 17:48:30.000000', '2015-11-08 17:50:33.000000', 0),
(7414994, 1168583, 47974, '<td valign="top">Not sure you will see this...........<br/> <br/><img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=98339" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">Ja-9</a> Thank you for your comments.  I am shooting µ4/3 so I get double the DoF of a full frame camera.  So I had the DoF in this shot like shooting a full frame camera at 8.0, so also the crop factor for µ4/3 is 2x and in full frame this would have been 420mm of reach (150mm lens + 1.4x TC = 210mm).I also did not want to go higher because I was already pushing my limit on ISO.  Oh, it is also the angle that causes the DoF to look narrower then it is.  This shot was taken at 4.0 also but was almost strait on so you can really see it better.<br/> <br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=1167307" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/2000-2999/2156/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1167307.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/2000-2999/2156/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1167307.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/2000-2999/2156/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1167307.jpg"/></a> <br/> <br/>[thumb]giantmike[/thumb] Thanks for your comments.  I thought about cropping some but ended up going with the full frame.  To also get the nose in focus (with the eyes) I would have had to shot at a minimum of 13.5 (would give 13 inches of DoF) which would have taken my ISO entirely to high for me.  I actually wanted to get at least the first set of side teeth in focus also but could not so I backed down to really draw attention to the eyes.</td>', 'Not sure you will see this...........  Ja-9 Thank you for your comments.  I am shooting µ4/3 so I get double the DoF of a full frame camera.  So I had the DoF in this shot like shooting a full frame camera at 8.0, so also the crop factor for µ4/3 is 2x and in full frame this would have been 420mm of reach (150mm lens + 1.4x TC = 210mm).I also did not want to go higher because I was already pushing my limit on ISO.  Oh, it is also the angle that causes the DoF to look narrower then it is.  This shot was taken at 4.0 also but was almost strait on so you can really see it better.   [thumb]giantmike[/thumb] Thanks for your comments.  I thought about cropping some but ended up going with the full frame.  To also get the nose in focus (with the eyes) I would have had to shot at a minimum of 13.5 (would give 13 inches of DoF) which would have taken my ISO entirely to high for me.  I actually wanted to get at least the first set of side teeth in focus also but could not so I backed down to really draw attention to the eyes.', '2015-11-08 17:51:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(7414997, 1168583, 136989, '<td valign="top">Personally I like the color version you posted better.  It somehow lessens the impact of the blurry snout for me.  Maybe the blur would have worked if the angle to the eye had been a bit different (i.e. the snout pointed more to the right edge than center.)  He definitely has that "I would love to eat you" glare going on.  I probably would''ve added a point or two if I had seen the comments about how close you were ... Yikes!  Stay safe out there.  :+)</td>', 'Personally I like the color version you posted better.  It somehow lessens the impact of the blurry snout for me.  Maybe the blur would have worked if the angle to the eye had been a bit different (i.e. the snout pointed more to the right edge than center.)  He definitely has that "I would love to eat you" glare going on.  I probably would''ve added a point or two if I had seen the comments about how close you were ... Yikes!  Stay safe out there.  :+)', '2015-11-08 18:10:49.000000', NULL, 0),
(7414998, 1169075, 50695, '<td valign="top">I love the way this picture doesn''t pop. I love it''s lack of pop. I love how it remains unpopped.</td>', 'I love the way this picture doesn''t pop. I love it''s lack of pop. I love how it remains unpopped.', '2015-11-08 18:40:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(7414999, 1168989, 30049, '<td valign="top">50th?!  Sub-6?  Wow.  FWIW, I thought it was a very well captured image.</td>', '50th?!  Sub-6?  Wow.  FWIW, I thought it was a very well captured image.', '2015-11-08 18:42:05.000000', NULL, 0),
(7415002, 1169089, 50695, '<td valign="top">7 from me. I like how the boats face off against the buildings. I really think it''s beautifully composed.</td>', '7 from me. I like how the boats face off against the buildings. I really think it''s beautifully composed.', '2015-11-08 18:44:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(7415003, 1169160, 50695, '<td valign="top">I gave this a 7 and I probably would have bumped it higher but I lost track of it. We all wear faces, one of us is just more obvious about it.</td>', 'I gave this a 7 and I probably would have bumped it higher but I lost track of it. We all wear faces, one of us is just more obvious about it.', '2015-11-08 18:45:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(7415004, 1169017, 50695, '<td valign="top">I like how you used the mirror thing to frame what''s below it. A good candid. 7 from me.</td>', 'I like how you used the mirror thing to frame what''s below it. A good candid. 7 from me.', '2015-11-08 18:46:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(7415005, 1168849, 50695, '<td valign="top">I like this blurry face-off. I feel like I''m watching closed circuit video of something significant. 7 from me.</td>', 'I like this blurry face-off. I feel like I''m watching closed circuit video of something significant. 7 from me.', '2015-11-08 18:47:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(7415016, 1168974, 98339, '<td valign="top">Arna, What your picture is lacking is luminosity/luster/lighting...your leaves were probably very vibrant when you saw this.  Are you shooting in RAW/JPEG?  That could be a major difference...with RAW you can play with light and color better.<br/> <br/>IMO you need some good Dodge &amp; Burn and I''d add a <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jGNctntIOx8" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">Soft Focus</a>.  It would make your colors really pop.<br/> <br/>I agree that possible with some "step photography" (that''s where you take a few steps left or right) to get the rocks and water to look as good as possible, getting rid of the tree/log.  It looks like you could have gone to the right and gotten in that little opening between the rocks and away from the log...therefore making a channel/line for your eyes to enter the picture and travel through the water and into the trees.  And personally I''d of sharpened this more (before the soft focus...by way of High Pass Filter (applied lightly).<br/> <br/>This all being said doesn''t mean that it''s not a good picture.  When I started here I just tried to "beat my score"....and did ALOT of tutorials on youtube!!!  Good luck...not bad for your 6 entry...FS''s are the toughest by far!!!  Enter more of the weekly challenges...they are more "focused" for subject.</td>', 'Arna, What your picture is lacking is luminosity/luster/lighting...your leaves were probably very vibrant when you saw this.  Are you shooting in RAW/JPEG?  That could be a major difference...with RAW you can play with light and color better. IMO you need some good Dodge & Burn and I''d add a Soft Focus.  It would make your colors really pop. I agree that possible with some "step photography" (that''s where you take a few steps left or right) to get the rocks and water to look as good as possible, getting rid of the tree/log.  It looks like you could have gone to the right and gotten in that little opening between the rocks and away from the log...therefore making a channel/line for your eyes to enter the picture and travel through the water and into the trees.  And personally I''d of sharpened this more (before the soft focus...by way of High Pass Filter (applied lightly). This all being said doesn''t mean that it''s not a good picture.  When I started here I just tried to "beat my score"....and did ALOT of tutorials on youtube!!!  Good luck...not bad for your 6 entry...FS''s are the toughest by far!!!  Enter more of the weekly challenges...they are more "focused" for subject.', '2015-11-08 20:01:50.000000', NULL, 0),
(7415133, 1167806, 159723, '<td valign="top">I understand that the waterfall is a hidden little treasure, which gives me an idea of how nice it probably was to be there in real life, but my initial thought when I first saw the image was as <img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=98339" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">Ja-9</a> pointed out - the waterfall being secondary to the big rock. The waterfall becomes too hidden in my opinion. That being said I like the nice autum colors and that the images is a different from many other waterfall images :) I''m sure it will look nice on your wall!</td>', 'I understand that the waterfall is a hidden little treasure, which gives me an idea of how nice it probably was to be there in real life, but my initial thought when I first saw the image was as  Ja-9 pointed out - the waterfall being secondary to the big rock. The waterfall becomes too hidden in my opinion. That being said I like the nice autum colors and that the images is a different from many other waterfall images :) I''m sure it will look nice on your wall!', '2015-11-09 02:14:42.000000', NULL, 0),
(7415137, 1168952, 61396, '<td valign="top">so what were they watching?</td>', 'so what were they watching?', '2015-11-09 02:55:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(7415150, 1169068, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Wow that IS cool!  Very dramatic scene.  Lots of good tones there in the water and sky, barely any blown-out highlights on the crests of the waves.  Hard to say how you could have shot this any better, considering the lens you were using, which would limit you somewhat.  If being higher up from the Well were an option, to allow a better view of the water pouring in, it could be worth trying to capture this again; such a location deserves a POV worthy of it :-)<br/> <br/>Hope this has been helpful, feel free to PM me with any questions<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Wow that IS cool!  Very dramatic scene.  Lots of good tones there in the water and sky, barely any blown-out highlights on the crests of the waves.  Hard to say how you could have shot this any better, considering the lens you were using, which would limit you somewhat.  If being higher up from the Well were an option, to allow a better view of the water pouring in, it could be worth trying to capture this again; such a location deserves a POV worthy of it :-) Hope this has been helpful, feel free to PM me with any questions Susan', '2015-11-09 07:37:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(7415151, 1168993, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Why this image did not finish in the top ten is beyond me.  Good composition with all kinds of interesting things to see like the iceberg to the right, fantastic spill of the Milky Way across the sky, their reflections in the water.  <br/> <br/>There is some noise in the water but when you''re shooting at ISO 3200 for 30 seconds esp on a surface that is moving...sometimes the voters here seem to have unrealistic expectations, in that they want everything to be perfectly tack-sharp. <br/> <br/>Anyway I really can''t find anything that wrong with this pic and you did finish well, so good work overall.<br/> <br/>Feel free to pm me<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Why this image did not finish in the top ten is beyond me.  Good composition with all kinds of interesting things to see like the iceberg to the right, fantastic spill of the Milky Way across the sky, their reflections in the water.   There is some noise in the water but when you''re shooting at ISO 3200 for 30 seconds esp on a surface that is moving...sometimes the voters here seem to have unrealistic expectations, in that they want everything to be perfectly tack-sharp.  Anyway I really can''t find anything that wrong with this pic and you did finish well, so good work overall. Feel free to pm me Susan', '2015-11-09 07:45:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(7415154, 1168952, 124815, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by mitalapo:</b></div><hr/><i> so what were they watching? </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Ah, they waited for a few minutes for a huge mirror to appear after a slew of adds so that they can look at themselves. Bunch of narcissists! Just kidding. But where could one see oneself so big over NYC?</td>', 'Originally posted by mitalapo: so what were they watching?   Ah, they waited for a few minutes for a huge mirror to appear after a slew of adds so that they can look at themselves. Bunch of narcissists! Just kidding. But where could one see oneself so big over NYC?', '2015-11-09 07:51:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(7415155, 1168502, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Isn''t this castle from either The Holy Grail or Princess Bride? Or both possibly; I''ve always enjoyed touring castles whenever I''m overseas.  Anyway nice shot but I think it could use some work.  I don''t know if that great mat of yellowed vegetation in the bottom right does much for the comp; it''s just there and partly swallowing the castle''s reflection, and draws the eye away from the castle.  I would love to have seen more of the ramp leading up to the castle from the right, but maybe there''s just a huge carpark full of tour buses there :-)  The light''s a little dull but that''s Britain for you.  I think more contrast would have helped to add some drama.<br/> <br/>Hope this has been helpful<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Isn''t this castle from either The Holy Grail or Princess Bride? Or both possibly; I''ve always enjoyed touring castles whenever I''m overseas.  Anyway nice shot but I think it could use some work.  I don''t know if that great mat of yellowed vegetation in the bottom right does much for the comp; it''s just there and partly swallowing the castle''s reflection, and draws the eye away from the castle.  I would love to have seen more of the ramp leading up to the castle from the right, but maybe there''s just a huge carpark full of tour buses there :-)  The light''s a little dull but that''s Britain for you.  I think more contrast would have helped to add some drama. Hope this has been helpful Susan', '2015-11-09 07:51:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(7415156, 1168502, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Isn''t this castle from either The Holy Grail or Princess Bride? Or both possibly; I''ve always enjoyed touring castles whenever I''m overseas.  Anyway nice shot but I think it could use some work.  I don''t know if that great mat of yellowed vegetation in the bottom right does much for the comp; it''s just there and partly swallowing the castle''s reflection, and draws the eye away from the castle.  I would love to have seen more of the ramp leading up to the castle from the right, but maybe there''s just a huge carpark full of tour buses there :-)  The light''s a little dull but that''s Britain for you.  I think more contrast would have helped to add some drama.<br/> <br/>Hope this has been helpful<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Isn''t this castle from either The Holy Grail or Princess Bride? Or both possibly; I''ve always enjoyed touring castles whenever I''m overseas.  Anyway nice shot but I think it could use some work.  I don''t know if that great mat of yellowed vegetation in the bottom right does much for the comp; it''s just there and partly swallowing the castle''s reflection, and draws the eye away from the castle.  I would love to have seen more of the ramp leading up to the castle from the right, but maybe there''s just a huge carpark full of tour buses there :-)  The light''s a little dull but that''s Britain for you.  I think more contrast would have helped to add some drama. Hope this has been helpful Susan', '2015-11-09 07:51:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(7415158, 1168974, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Hmm it looks like other post-commenters have already picked out the best and worst of this shot so I really don''t have anything new to add.  I do agree that you mainly need a focal point, something to draw the eye; my eye is wandering looking for something.  It''s like seeing a set before the actors arrive.  The sky is a bit bland.  I would like to see the yellows pop a little more. The trees on the left could easily be cropped out as they aren''t adding to the comp, they''re just there.<br/> <br/>As has been noted already, FS are by hardest the best to do well in as everyone enters something different.  More weekly challenges where you are given more directions (usually anyway) may help.<br/> <br/>Hope this has been useful<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Hmm it looks like other post-commenters have already picked out the best and worst of this shot so I really don''t have anything new to add.  I do agree that you mainly need a focal point, something to draw the eye; my eye is wandering looking for something.  It''s like seeing a set before the actors arrive.  The sky is a bit bland.  I would like to see the yellows pop a little more. The trees on the left could easily be cropped out as they aren''t adding to the comp, they''re just there. As has been noted already, FS are by hardest the best to do well in as everyone enters something different.  More weekly challenges where you are given more directions (usually anyway) may help. Hope this has been useful Susan', '2015-11-09 07:59:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(7415160, 1168952, 61396, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by mariuca:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by mitalapo:</b></div><hr/><i> so what were they watching? </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Ah, they waited for a few minutes for a huge mirror to appear after a slew of adds so that they can look at themselves. Bunch of narcissists! Just kidding. But where could one see oneself so big over NYC? </i></td></tr></table> <br/>Cool: they would have seen something like your image here.</td>', 'Originally posted by mariuca: Originally posted by mitalapo: so what were they watching?   Ah, they waited for a few minutes for a huge mirror to appear after a slew of adds so that they can look at themselves. Bunch of narcissists! Just kidding. But where could one see oneself so big over NYC?  Cool: they would have seen something like your image here.', '2015-11-09 08:10:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(7415184, 1167806, 156466, '<td valign="top">It''s a wonderful scene, but there''s too much pulling my eye away from the waterfall even after I see it.  The easiest way to understand what needs to be fixed is to view the entire image and then slowly scroll down to the comments - when 1/3 the image disappears all of a sudden it pops.  You could leave it the same width and you''d be fine.  5 as is for me, but there''s a 7 or 8 in there.</td>', 'It''s a wonderful scene, but there''s too much pulling my eye away from the waterfall even after I see it.  The easiest way to understand what needs to be fixed is to view the entire image and then slowly scroll down to the comments - when 1/3 the image disappears all of a sudden it pops.  You could leave it the same width and you''d be fine.  5 as is for me, but there''s a 7 or 8 in there.', '2015-11-09 09:00:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(7415185, 1169126, 135744, '<td valign="top">This is a superb photo and I agree with the Facebook crowd. I would be very pleased if I had taken a photo like this.</td>', 'This is a superb photo and I agree with the Facebook crowd. I would be very pleased if I had taken a photo like this.', '2015-11-09 09:02:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(7415187, 1169126, 156466, '<td valign="top">It''s a really nice shot and nicely processed, but for me it feels upside-down in all the ways you don''t want.  If that''s the way it was shot then it needs to be flipped, and if you flipped it you shouldn''t have.  I don''t know how much more the flip would make a difference for me - probably a point, but this may have wound up a 6 either way for me in a FS.</td>', 'It''s a really nice shot and nicely processed, but for me it feels upside-down in all the ways you don''t want.  If that''s the way it was shot then it needs to be flipped, and if you flipped it you shouldn''t have.  I don''t know how much more the flip would make a difference for me - probably a point, but this may have wound up a 6 either way for me in a FS.', '2015-11-09 09:06:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(7415190, 1167806, 159723, '<td valign="top">What <img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=156466" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">backdoorhippie</a> said made total sense for me. I scrolled down and felt urged to had an attempt at cropping your image (just from a screen shot). I hope you don''t mind, Mike. I just thought I might learn something as well :) <br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=1169613" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/155000-159999/159723/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1169613.png'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/155000-159999/159723/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1169613.png'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/155000-159999/159723/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1169613.png"/></a> <br/>By all means not implying that it is better than the original, but this was a crop that I liked.</td>', 'What  backdoorhippie said made total sense for me. I scrolled down and felt urged to had an attempt at cropping your image (just from a screen shot). I hope you don''t mind, Mike. I just thought I might learn something as well :)  By all means not implying that it is better than the original, but this was a crop that I liked.', '2015-11-09 09:32:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(7415196, 1168905, 38032, '<td valign="top">Gave this beauty a 9.</td>', 'Gave this beauty a 9.', '2015-11-09 09:46:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(7415206, 1168713, 156466, '<td valign="top">Responding to your request for feedback, I don''t know that I can help you understand why it didn''t do better.  It''s a brilliant portrait.  If I''m being picky I might have fixed the mascara fleck below her left eye, and perhaps removed the red spot by the eye brow.  But again, I''m being picky.  I did not vote this time, but if I had it would have been a 7 for me, maybe an 8.  Free studies tend to be a wallpaper show and shots like this are often tragically under-rewarded.</td>', 'Responding to your request for feedback, I don''t know that I can help you understand why it didn''t do better.  It''s a brilliant portrait.  If I''m being picky I might have fixed the mascara fleck below her left eye, and perhaps removed the red spot by the eye brow.  But again, I''m being picky.  I did not vote this time, but if I had it would have been a 7 for me, maybe an 8.  Free studies tend to be a wallpaper show and shots like this are often tragically under-rewarded.', '2015-11-09 10:44:28.000000', NULL, 0),
(7415208, 1168974, 123833, '<td valign="top">I think the biggest problem is the composition. In the landscape there does not need to be focal point but then you need to look at shapes and layers. I played with your uncropped image and thought that there were distinct layers and that the sky reflection would be good emphasize as well as the foreground rocks. The sky is a problem, did you really shoot in JPEG and not in RAW? With your gear it seems such a pity. The sky could be saved. Here is my PP idea:<br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=1169617" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/120000-124999/123833/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1169617.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/120000-124999/123833/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1169617.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/120000-124999/123833/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1169617.jpg"/></a></td>', 'I think the biggest problem is the composition. In the landscape there does not need to be focal point but then you need to look at shapes and layers. I played with your uncropped image and thought that there were distinct layers and that the sky reflection would be good emphasize as well as the foreground rocks. The sky is a problem, did you really shoot in JPEG and not in RAW? With your gear it seems such a pity. The sky could be saved. Here is my PP idea:', '2015-11-09 10:49:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(7415211, 1167806, 114225, '<td valign="top">Great comments!<br/> <br/>I really do see what you mean about that tighter crop. That helps a lot.</td>', 'Great comments! I really do see what you mean about that tighter crop. That helps a lot.', '2015-11-09 10:56:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(7415213, 1168713, 159723, '<td valign="top">I saw your request for comments. I think you may have made it really hard for yourself to get a high score, as this image is similar to "a shot" that has been done extremely well many times before. I think what separates your image from the others is that the eyes are not as distinct in yours. I think adding a bit of color, for example a little green, could make the eyes pop a bit more. Or at least just adding a dark line around the iris, if you are not into manipulating the model''s features. Two DPC-images that came to mind when I saw your image, which instantly draws me in due to the focus on the eyes is<br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=1057182" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/1000-1999/1718/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1057182.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/1000-1999/1718/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1057182.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/1000-1999/1718/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1057182.jpg"/></a> by <img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=20608" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">MAK</a> and<br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=64527" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/0-999/198/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_64527.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/0-999/198/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_64527.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/0-999/198/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_64527.jpg"/></a> by <img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=5728" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">kiwiness</a> <br/>Both have very vibrant eye color and an enhanced dark line around the iris</td>', 'I saw your request for comments. I think you may have made it really hard for yourself to get a high score, as this image is similar to "a shot" that has been done extremely well many times before. I think what separates your image from the others is that the eyes are not as distinct in yours. I think adding a bit of color, for example a little green, could make the eyes pop a bit more. Or at least just adding a dark line around the iris, if you are not into manipulating the model''s features. Two DPC-images that came to mind when I saw your image, which instantly draws me in due to the focus on the eyes is by  MAK and by  kiwiness Both have very vibrant eye color and an enhanced dark line around the iris', '2015-11-09 10:57:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(7415214, 1168713, 96751, '<td valign="top">While the technicals are all there (great light, good color, tonal range, etc.), there are some details that don''t work, and as others have mentioned in the thread, it is far from an original idea.<br/> <br/>The details I refer to, and I am being picky, are the texture of the wrap, the way it cuts off her camera left eye, showing too much forehead, stray hairs on the right, etc.<br/> <br/>These "classic" images really have to be off the wall perfect or bring something different to them in order to rise above the clutter.</td>', 'While the technicals are all there (great light, good color, tonal range, etc.), there are some details that don''t work, and as others have mentioned in the thread, it is far from an original idea. The details I refer to, and I am being picky, are the texture of the wrap, the way it cuts off her camera left eye, showing too much forehead, stray hairs on the right, etc. These "classic" images really have to be off the wall perfect or bring something different to them in order to rise above the clutter.', '2015-11-09 10:57:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(7415217, 1168713, 114225, '<td valign="top">I gave you a 7, and am surprised you didn''t score higher.<br/> <br/>- Great catch lights in the eyes<br/>- Perfect focus<br/>- Really good colors<br/> <br/>If I have to find flaws, I think it would be<br/>- The vertical orientation can be hard to score well with. Sometimes cropping so it''s not so vertical can help<br/>- I don''t like eye liner. Everything else feels natural about this.<br/> <br/></td>', 'I gave you a 7, and am surprised you didn''t score higher. - Great catch lights in the eyes- Perfect focus- Really good colors If I have to find flaws, I think it would be- The vertical orientation can be hard to score well with. Sometimes cropping so it''s not so vertical can help- I don''t like eye liner. Everything else feels natural about this. ', '2015-11-09 11:05:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(7415247, 1168989, 163081, '<td valign="top">someone really didn''t like the picture.... a 1<br/>Btw. this is not a staged shot.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2015-11-09 14:21:26.</i></td>', 'someone really didn''t like the picture.... a 1Btw. this is not a staged shot.', '2015-11-09 14:21:10.000000', '2015-11-09 14:21:26.000000', 0),
